.TH MAIL 1
.SH NAME
mail \- send or receive mail among users
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mail
person ...
.br
.B mail
[
.B \-r
] [
.B \-q
] [
.B \-p
] [
.B \-f
file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mail
with no argument prints a user's mail, message-by-message, in last-in,
first-out order; the optional argument
.B \-r
causes first-in, first-out
order.
If the
.B \-p
flag is given, the mail is printed with no questions
asked; otherwise, for each message,
.B mail
reads a line from the standard
input to direct disposition of the message.
.TP 8
newline
Go on to next message.
.TP
.B d
Delete message and go on to the next.
.TP
.B p
Print message again.
.TP
\-
Go back to previous message.
.TP
\fBs\fR [ \fIfile\fR ] ...
Save the message in the named
.I files
(`mbox' default).
.TP
\fBw\fR [ \fIfile\fR ] ...
Save the message, without a header, in the named
.I files
(`mbox'
default).
.TP
\fBm\fR [ \fIperson\fR ] ...
Mail the message to the named
.I persons
(yourself is default).
.TP
\fBEOT\fR (control\-D)
Put unexamined mail back in the mailbox and stop.
.TP
.B q
Same as EOT.
.TP
.B x
Exit, without changing the mailbox file.
.TP
.BI ! command
Escape to the Shell to do
.IR command .
.TP
.B ?
Print a command summary.
.PP
An interrupt stops the printing of the current letter.
The optional argument
.B \-q
causes
.B mail
to exit after interrupts without changing the mailbox.
.PP
When
.I persons
are named,
.B mail
takes the standard input up to an end-of-file
(or a line with just `.') and adds it to each
.IR person 's
`mail' file.
The message is preceded by the sender's name and a postmark.
Lines
that look like postmarks are prepended with `>'.
A
.I person
is usually a
user name recognized by
.BR login (1).
On installations with
.B uucp
communications
software, a recipient on a remote system can be specified by
prefixing
.I person
by the system name and exclamation mark (see
.BR uucp (1)).
.PP
The
.B \-f
option causes the named file, e.g. `mbox', to be printed as if it
were the mail file.
.PP
Each user owns his own mailbox, which is by default generally readable
but not writable.
The command does not delete an empty mailbox nor
change its mode, so a user may make it unreadable if desired.
.PP
When a user logs in he is informed of the presence of mail.
.SH FILES
.nf
.ta 1.4i
/usr/spool/mail/*	mailboxes
/etc/passwd	to identify sender and locate persons
mbox	saved mail
/tmp/ma*	temp file
dead.letter	unmailable text
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
Mail the contents of file
.I xletter
to
.I fred
and
.IR joe :
.IP
mail fred joe < xletter
.PP
Send a letter to
.IR eve ;
the letter's contents are read from the terminal:
.PP
.RS
.nf
mail eve
Madam, I'm Adam.
^D
.fi
.RE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
write(1)
.SH BUGS
There is a locking mechanism intended to prevent two senders from
accessing the same mailbox, but it is not perfect and deadly embraces are
possible.
.sp
